WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.702
Hardcoded secrets are one of the most common and avoidable C# security anti-patterns.

2
00:00:05.702 --> 00:00:15.496
Secrets include passwords, connection strings, API keys, tokens, certificates, private keys, signing keys, storage keys, and provider credentials.

3
00:00:15.496 --> 00:00:21.936
They are not ordinary configuration values because exposure can grant access or undermine trust.

4
00:00:21.936 --> 00:00:36.627
Secrets should not appear in source code, committed appsettings files, sample projects, test fixtures, screenshots, documentation, logs, CI output, deployment artifacts, support tickets, or copied troubleshooting notes.

5
00:00:36.627 --> 00:00:44.342
Those locations are replicated, indexed, retained, and shared in ways that are difficult to control after the fact.

6
00:00:44.342 --> 00:00:49.105
Development-only convenience must not become production secret storage.

7
00:00:49.105 --> 00:01:01.850
A local secret file, a temporary environment variable, or a shared test credential may be acceptable inside a controlled development workflow, but that does not make it a production pattern.

8
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:08.223
Production secrets need access control, inventory, ownership, rotation, and operational review.

9
00:01:08.223 --> 00:01:10.303
Environment separation matters.

10
00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:20.231
Development, test, staging, production, and customer-specific deployments should not casually share credentials or security-sensitive configuration.

11
00:01:20.231 --> 00:01:28.952
Shared secrets increase blast radius, make testing less trustworthy, and make rotation harder when one environment needs a change.

12
00:01:28.952 --> 00:01:41.161
Configuration drift occurs when production behavior differs from reviewed expectations because settings were copied, overridden, forgotten, or changed outside the normal review path.

13
00:01:41.161 --> 00:01:51.961
A secure code path can behave unsafely if the deployed configuration disables a control, points to the wrong service, or uses a broader credential than intended.

14
00:01:51.961 --> 00:02:06.384
Safer habits include controlled secret stores, managed identity where appropriate, environment-aware configuration, documented ownership, rotation schedules, redaction rules, startup validation, and periodic review.

15
00:02:06.384 --> 00:02:15.574
The key habit is treating configuration as part of the security design rather than a pile of values that only matters at deployment time.
